nd for isotropic log-concave probability measures. In its acknowledgements, the authors say that most proofs and mathematical ideas were found by ChatGPT; they identify the suspension construction as a notable exception suggested by the authors and say their role was mainly to understand the proofs and improve their exposition. This is recorded as an AI-assisted candidate theorem, not as an independently validated or accepted theorem: the source is a fresh preprint, and no external reconstruction or peer review was found at ingestion. Update (2026-10-16): The World Won't Wait: Evaluating and Teaching Agents Under a Live Clock. arXiv:2610.20665v1 Announce Type: new Abstract: Large language model (LLM) agents can solve complex interactive tasks, yet reasoning and tool calls take time. \n## Sources\n\nprimary sources\n\n  * Presenting a proof of the Kannan–Lovász–Simonovits conjecture\n\narXiv · preprint · 4 Oct 2026\n\nother sources\n\n  * So much progress in Math!!\n\nr/accelerate · other · 6 Oct 2026\n\nSecondary domains: Computer Science\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPHYSICAL REVIEW E 111, 044134 (2025) (https://journals.aps.org/pre/pdf/10.1103/PhysRevE.111.044134)\nciteturn2search15 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.4 years ago; low the same arcsine law pi(ti|t) = 1/[π√ti(t −ti)] for ti = ... tumble processes [58–60], permutation-generated random\ncomputed by Lévy [34] and, quite remarkably, they all fol-\nlow the same arcsine law pi(ti|t) = 1/[π√ti(t −ti)] for ti =\nto, tm, or tl, where the name originates from the correspond-\ning arcsine cumulative distribution, i.e., Prob [ti ⩽T |t] =\n(2/π) sin−1(√T/t).\nThese three quantities are relevant in various complex sys-\ntems and have been studied for a wide variety of stochastic\nprocesses with diverse applications. The occupation time to\nhas been analyzed (e.g., for random jump processes [35], re-\nnewal and resetting processes [36–39], and Gaussian Markov\nprocesses [40,41]) and has played an important role in the\ncontext of persistence in nonequilibrium systems [23,42–45].\nFurthermore, it has been studied for various other processes\nsuch as opinions in voter models [46], blinking nanocrystals\n[47], diffusion in a disordered environment [48–51], a class\nof spin glass models [52], sub- and superdiffusive processes\n[53–56], random acceleration processes [57], active run-and-\ntumble processes [58–60], permutation-generated random\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nrandom — arXiv Combinatorics (https://www.arxiv.symmetricfunctions.com/keyword/random?page=13)\nciteturn2search3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; That the answer (namely, $4^{-n}\\binom{2n}{n}$) is independent of $μ$ follows from a classical theorem of Darling, stating that for symmetric atomless increments, the distribution of each individual rank in the permutation obtained by ranking the partial sums is independent of the step law. We give a self-contained proof and extend the result to the permutations generated by partial sums of uniformly random signed permutations of any fixed, finite, generic set of reals. ... Finally, we exhibit an infinite family of constructions whose limiting permutons interpolate between the Wiener permuton and the recursive separable permuton; each has the same intensity permuton, providing a single two-dimensional extension of the classical arcsine law for all of them.\n\n2026-09-21 v2\n\nPeter E. Francis, Peter Winkler\n\n# • INFO • BIB • PDF • ARXIV •\n\nPermutations from Random Walk\n\nXavier and Yushi run a \"random race\" as follows. An atomless probability distribution $μ$ on the real line is chosen. The runners begin at zero. At time $i$ Xavier draws $\\mathbf{X}_i$ from $μ$ and advances that distance, while Yushi advances by an independent drawing $\\mathbf{Y}_i$. After $n$ such moves, what is the probability that Yushi led all the way? That the answer (namely, $4^{-n}\\binom{2n}{n}$) is independent of $μ$ follows from a classical theorem of Darling, stating that for symmetric atomless increments, the distribution of each individual rank in the permutation obtained by ranking the partial sums is independent of the step law. We give a self-contained proof and extend the result to the permutations generated by partial sums of uniformly random signed permutations of any fixed, finite, generic set of reals. For atomless increments with mean zero and finite variance, without assuming symmetry, we show that random-walk permutations approach a random object that we call the \"Wiener permuton,\" whose expected pattern densities equal the probabilities of the corresponding permutations generated by finite random walks with centered Laplace increments. Finally, we exhibit an infinite family of constructions whose limiting permutons interpolate between the Wiener permuton and the recursive separable permuton; each has the same intensity permuton, providing a single two-dimensional extension of the classical arcsine law for all of them.\n\npermuton probability distribution random random walk signed signed permutation permutation\n\n2026-09-21 v2\n\nAldo Kiem, Olaf Parczyk, Christoph Spiegel\n\n# • INFO • BIB • PDF • ARXIV •\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nConservation status of New Zealand Araneae (spiders), 2020 (https://www.doc.govt.nz/globalassets/documents/science-and-technical/nztcs34entire.pdf)\nciteturn2search16 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5.2 years ago; Taxa whose natural presence in New Zealand is either discontinuous (Migrant) or sporadic or temporary (Vagrant) or which have succeeded in recently (since 1950) establishing a resident breeding population (Coloniser). ... Taxa that have become naturalised in the wild after being deliberately or accidentally introduced into New Zealand by human agency. ... Sirvid et al. – Conservation status of New Zealand Araneae (spiders), 2020\n\nA 5000–20000 mature individuals; population stable (±10%)  \nB >20 000 mature individuals; population stable or increasing at >10%\n\nThe range of a relictual taxon takes into account the area currently occupied as a ratio of its former extent. Relict can also include taxa that exist as reintroduced and self-sustaining populations within or outside their former known range (for more details see Townsend et al. (2008)).\n\n*At Risk – Naturally Uncommon*\n\nTaxa whose distribution is confined to a specific geographical area or which occur within naturally small and widely scattered populations, where this distribution is not the result of human disturbance.\n\nNon-resident Native\n\nTaxa whose natural presence in New Zealand is either discontinuous (Migrant) or sporadic or temporary (Vagrant) or which have succeeded in recently (since 1950) establishing a resident breeding population (Coloniser).\n\n*Non-resident Native – Vagrant*\n\nTaxa whose occurrences, though natural, are sporadic and typically transitory, or migrants with fewer than 15 individuals visiting New Zealand per annum.\n\nNot Threatened\n\nResident native taxa that have large, stable populations.\n\nIntroduced and Naturalised\n\nTaxa that have become naturalised in the wild after being deliberately or accidentally introduced into New Zealand by human agency.\n\n32  \nSirvid et al. – Conservation status of New Zealand Araneae (spiders), 2020--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAfter the Enormous Theorem: Verification, Discovery, and the Missing Layer of Understanding – Random Bits of Knowledge (https://4m4.it/posts/after-the-enormous-theorem/index.html)\nciteturn2search4 [wordlim: 200] Published: last week; Crawled: 2 days ago; On the same day, OpenAI announced AI-generated results in harmonic analysis, theoretical computer science, and quantum field theory, together with an experiment in academic stewardship of machine-produced research. ... On 26 October 2026, Axiom announced what it described as the first complete computer-checked proof of the classification of finite simple groups (CFSG), a theorem established through a vast twentieth-century research programme. ... On that same date, OpenAI announced three ostensibly new results: a proof of Sogge’s local smoothing conjecture for the Euclidean wave equation in all spatial dimensions; a deterministic subexponential-time algorithm that would refute the Exponential Time Hypothesis (ETH); and existence constructions in four-dimensional quantum field theory, including pure Yang–Mills theory and quantum chromodynamics.\nAuthor\n\nAntonio Montano\n\nPublished\n\nOctober 27, 2026\n\nModified\n\nOctober 31, 2026\n\nAbstract\n\nOn 26 October 2026, Axiom announced a Lean formalization of the classification of finite simple groups, an established theorem whose human proof spans thousands of pages. On the same day, OpenAI announced AI-generated results in harmonic analysis, theoretical computer science, and quantum field theory, together with an experiment in academic stewardship of machine-produced research. On 27 October, Anthropic publicly documented a concurrent, separately developed claim to refute ETH. These events invoke formal verification but expose different problems: reconstructing an existing mathematical edifice and discovering claims that require new mathematical interpretation.\n\nThis article analyzes the mathematical substance and the exact documented scope of the results, distinguishes deductive verification from semantic fidelity and physical interpretation, and evaluates the KITP Yang–Mills committee’s staged release against AGMAI’s criticism of privileged access. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCloudflare introduced Clairic, an... | Oct 20 (https://devoured.fyi/digest-swipe/devoured-october-20-2026/)\nciteturn2search5 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2 weeks ago; Crawled: yesterday; The first version, dated September 30, 2026, claimed to show that all nontrivial zeros must be between 1/8 and 7/8, and was formally verified using the Lean programming language. ... In retrospect, Radziwiłł said, mathematicians who study the branch of number theory that all this work belongs to were mistaken in thinking that their tools weren’t strong enough to resolve the quasi-Riemann hypothesis. ... There are scores of results across not just number theory but also geometry and theoretical computer science that the proofs of quasi-Riemann will, in a swift stroke, establish as mathematical truths.\n\n## Diamonds in the Slop\n\nThis brings us to the quasi-Riemann hypothesis. Where the Riemann hypothesis posits that all the nontrivial zeros are on the vertical line at 1/2, the “quasi” version just asks one to prove that they are all contained in a vertical strip that’s narrower than the known strip between zero and 1. The narrower the strip, the more one can say about how the primes are distributed.\n\nOpenAI announced two different versions of their quasi-Riemann hypothesis proof. The first version, dated September 30, 2026, claimed to show that all nontrivial zeros must be between 1/8 and 7/8, and was formally verified using the Lean programming language. The second version, which was completed almost a week later (and is not yet formally verified), claimed to prove a weaker result: that the zeros must have a real part between 1/12 and 11/12. But while the first paper is 199 pages long, the second weighs in at a comparatively modest 49 pages.\n\nAs with many recent AI-generated results, both papers “are horribly written (in a way that would lead to immediate rejection at any mathematical journal),” Radziwiłł said in an email. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nArcsine laws for permutation generated random walks (https://sms.wgtn.ac.nz/foswiki/pub/Events/WWPMS2019/WebHome/HanLiang_Gan_2019.pdf)\nciteturn2search17 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6.9 years ago; Suppose X ∼ U[0, 10], and are independent of Y ∼ U[0, 10]. i i Han Liang Gan | Arcsine laws for PGRWs | 8 / 17 --- | --- | ---\nSimulation i.i.d. i.i.d. Suppose X ∼ U[0, 10], and are independent of Y ∼ U[0, 10]. i i Han Liang Gan | Arcsine laws for PGRWs | 8 / 17 --- | --- | -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nJorō Spiders (https://extension.psu.edu/joro-spiders)\nciteturn2search6 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Additionally, sporadic records include individuals from Pennsylvania, New Jersey, New York, and even southern Canada, although these spiders probably ballooned north as spiderlings and likely do no represent reproducing populations. The species was suggested as a model organism to track the impacts of climate change as it is expected to continue to move northward as average temperatures increase.\nPrior to 2000, the species was not recorded north of southern North Carolina. It has since apparently moved northward into southern Virginia. Additionally, sporadic records include individuals from Pennsylvania, New Jersey, New York, and even southern Canada, although these spiders probably ballooned north as spiderlings and likely do no represent reproducing populations. The species was suggested as a model organism to track the impacts of climate change as it is expected to continue to move northward as average temperatures increase.\n\nGolden silk orb-weavers are similar in size to jorō spiders. However, they can be distinguished based on the coloration. Female golden silk orb-weavers have a prosoma that is similarly covered in silvery hairs but the opisthosoma is dull orange to tan and covered with yellow spots. Additionally, the legs are brown with orange bands and have exaggerated hair brushes on legs I, II, and IV that look like fuzzy leg warmers.\n\nFigure 7. Adult female golden silk orb-weaver. Note the differences in coloration and fuzzy leg warmers on legs I, II, and IV that distinguish it from